<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Asaf-ud-Daula</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1748–1797</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Asaf-ud-Daula was Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> from 1775 until his death in 1797. He moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faizabad">Faizabad</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a>, which he transformed with monuments such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bara-imambara">Bara Imambara</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a> Darwaza, built partly as famine relief from 1784. His court attracted poets and artists, but he ceded territory and revenue to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, which increasingly controlled his state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/asaf-ud-daula.jpg" alt="Asaf-ud-Daula" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Originally a local artist c. 1780 copying a painting by Zoffany · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asifportrait2_-_Asuf_ud_Daula.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
